package cosmonic:kafka-plugin@0.1.0;

/// Kafka capability, built as a host component plugin.
///
/// The plugin is a single long-lived, host-scoped instance. Ephemeral workloads
/// call into it across store boundaries; it owns the Kafka connections, broker
/// metadata, and consumer-group state that no per-request instance could hold.
/// This is the same split AWS Lambda draws between an event poller and a
/// function — the difference is that here the poller is itself a sandboxed
/// component rather than privileged platform infrastructure.
///
/// Transport is the native Kafka wire protocol over TCP. Rust's `std::net`
/// lowers to `wasi:sockets` on `wasm32-wasip2`, and the plugin loader leaves
/// outbound connect unrestricted (it denies only `tcp-bind`/`udp-bind`), so no
/// import declaration is needed here — the toolchain adds the `wasi:sockets`
/// imports when the component is built.
world kafka-plugin {
    /// Broker addresses and topic settings, from the plugin's own `config:`
    /// block in the host manifest. This is the *only* channel for them: a
    /// plugin store is built without an environment, so `wasi:cli/environment`
    /// is present but always empty, and the host deliberately never writes the
    /// plugin's config to a file it could read. The host resolves this import
    /// against its native `wasi:config` plugin when it loads this one.
    import wasi:config/store@0.2.0-rc.1;

    /// Kafka-native surface: keys, partitions, and offsets survive.
    ///
    /// Every function is `async func`, and that is a requirement rather than a
    /// style choice: a plugin's capabilities are installed on a caller's linker
    /// as concurrent host functions, so a sync-declared function fails to bind
    /// with "type mismatch with async".
    ///
    /// That is also why there is no `wasmcloud:messaging/consumer` export here.
    /// The upstream 0.2.0 package declares plain `func`, so a workload importing
    /// it from this plugin would fail to link. A drop-in publish path needs the
    /// interface redeclared async — which makes it a different package from the
    /// one already-written workloads import, and so not a drop-in at all.
    export cosmonic:kafka/producer@0.1.0;
    export cosmonic:kafka/consumer@0.1.0;
    export cosmonic:kafka/types@0.1.0;

    /// The push direction. `handler` is imported for a *workload* to export:
    /// the host matches it to a bound workload and routes each call there, the
    /// reverse of the capability edges above.
    ///
    /// `wasmcloud:host/workload-call` is how the loop addresses one. A capability
    /// call can dispatch back to its own caller with no handle at all, but
    /// `wasi:cli/run` has no caller to inherit — it *is* the event source — so
    /// it must name a target explicitly.
    import cosmonic:kafka/handler@0.1.0;
    import wasmcloud:host/workload-call@0.1.3;

    /// The loop's brake. Without an await that actually suspends, a pass that
    /// finds nothing to do — no records, or no workload to hand them to — spins
    /// and starves the capability calls sharing this store.
    import wasi:clocks/monotonic-clock@0.3.0;

    /// The trigger runs with nobody to return an error to, so without this a
    /// permanent failure — an unreadable topic, a handler that always rejects —
    /// is indistinguishable from an idle one. It cost 240 seconds of silence to
    /// learn that once.
    import wasi:logging/logging@0.1.0-draft;

    /// The trigger loop itself: poll the broker, hand each batch to a workload,
    /// commit only once the workload says it is done. Off unless the `trigger`
    /// config key says otherwise, so a deployment that only wants the pull
    /// interface above pays nothing for this.
    export wasi:cli/run@0.3.0;
}
